>> title: Dried Bok Choy and Pork Lung Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork lung, dried bok choy, honey dates, apricot kernels, cantonese, old-fire soup, autumn, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 菜乾蜜棗豬肺湯, coi gon mat zou zyu fai tong, choi gon mat jou jue fai tong, dried bok choy and pork lung soup, sun-dried cabbage and pig lung soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 4 hr 30 min
>> slack: unforgiving — a lung that goes into the pot still pink and foaming perfumes the whole thing, and there is no washing it back out afterwards; the washing is most of an hour and it is the recipe
>> step.1: Autumn — 清熱潤燥, the dry season. This pot sits below the others on the shelf for one reason: the lung is washed under running water, squeezed out and refilled again and again, until the water runs clear and the whole thing is white. That is most of an hour for one person and twenty minutes for another, and nobody can honestly put a number on it. Everything after it is the easiest pot here.
>> step.2: wash it white, then brown it dry
>> step.3: blanch from cold, then rinse the shin
>> step.4: soak the dried cabbage 30 min
>> step.5: simmer 3 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.6: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.7: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

Autumn — 清熱潤燥, the dry season. This pot sits below the others on the shelf for one reason: the lung is washed under running water, squeezed out and refilled again and again, until the water runs clear and the whole thing is white. That is most of an hour for one person and twenty minutes for another, and nobody can honestly put a number on it. Everything after it is the easiest pot here.

Wash @pork lung{1}(zyu fai, whole, windpipe attached) under @running water{}, letting it fill and squeezing it out, over and over, until the water runs clear and the lung is white rather than pink. Then cut it into thick pieces and turn them in a dry #wok{} over a high flame until the last of the water and the foam has gone.

Blanch @pork shin{1/2%lb}(225 g) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse it under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Soak @dried bok choy{3%oz}(85 g; coi gon, whole sun-dried cabbage — for autumn dryness) in cold water, ~soak{30%min}, then wash it fold by fold, since the grit sits in the heart of it, and rinse @honey dates{3}(mat zou; the sweetening, and the word used is 潤) and @apricot kernels{1/4%cup}(naam bak hang; sweet and bitter together, about three to one — the bitter kind goes in small and always cooked through, never raw) with it.

Simmer @&(~3)browned lung{} with @&(~2)blanched shin{}, @&(~1)cabbage and dried goods{} and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{3%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
